#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1380.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101908} :activity/id #uuid "e770d350-fe93-58e5-a551-254f24aea748" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049280 :topic/id #uuid "e992dbdf-067e-5347-82bc-a58321ce8275" :topic/math-academy-id 1203} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102034 :tutorial/content "The region $\\textrm{R},$ shown below, is the area under the graph of $f(x) = 2x+1$ over the interval $[1,3].$ What is the height of the rectangle defined over the same interval that has the same area as $\\textrm{R}?$ \n\n![](images/01/01597200b1f0e147fa8c53b6301d0c8e37208b5a6cce64cf0d9859d1220d8385.png)\n\n \n\nUsing the formula for the area of a trapezoid, we have that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\textrm{Area}\\,(\\textrm{R}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}(3+7)(3-1)= 10.}\n$$\n\nFor the rectangle to have the same area, we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =f(c)(3-1) \\\\ f(c) & =5 \\\\ 2c+1 & =5 \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize what we've just done by saying that\n\n$$\n\\int_1^3 (2x +1)\\, \\textrm d x= f(2) \\cdot (3-1).\n$$\n\nThis is an example of the **mean value theorem for integrals**, which states:\n\n*If $f(x)$ is a continuous function on the closed interval $[a, b]$, then there exists a number $c$ in that interval such that*\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x = f(c) \\cdot (b-a).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c1b3241c-c8fd-514f-aa8c-a000710896d4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 986 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d75b1c61-1292-5625-8252-d8c10bae2e1b" :step/math-academy-id 618980 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101909}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101021 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100733 :question/id #uuid "a0f21b4c-0f5c-5567-aaec-9da481d2e7ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-547"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86221215-32a6-5c5a-bcd7-220e166bcee8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050819}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459274}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Integrals"} :step/id #uuid "3e6aeea7-0134-5101-a19a-c37ab846bb82" :step/math-academy-id 618981 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101910}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101910 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102035 :tutorial/content "The **average value** of a continuous function on a closed interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_\\textrm{avg} = \\dfrac{1}{b-a}\\int_a^b f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1cced554-b7fa-5fd4-b8b5-86c8e0bb4bc0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 988 :tutorial/title "The Average Value of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "42fd3643-e43b-5a23-8553-5171fa424230" :step/math-academy-id 618984 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101911}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101911 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101022 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100734 :question/id #uuid "0c84fc6d-422e-5729-94d2-a12cda71f78d" :question/math-academy-id "e-548"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55fab3ac-cad3-54bd-b07b-9159554b634c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297310} {:db/id #edb/ref 175921862973